<!--
    GH-854 (kuna): on AArch64 a value a function leaves in x8 before a call is
    printed as that call's first argument.  x8 is the indirect-result register,
    the cspec's `hiddenret` input entry, so checkInputTrialUse scores a value
    the caller wrote there and never read again as the hidden return-buffer
    pointer of a struct-returning callee, and its own resource section sorts
    it ahead of x0.  x8 is also a scratch register, so a syscall number or any
    leftover value becomes the call's first argument.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring".  The P0 assertion
    `option hiddenretarg on|off` decides whether that trial is scored no-use
    when the callee never takes x8 (no path reads it before writing it, none
    leaves for unread code with it unwritten) or x8 holds a constant in the
    null page (below 0x1000).

    The fixture, hand assembled:
      f     mov x8,#0x38; mov x0,#3; bl foo   foo is `add x0,x0,#1; ret`
      f2    add x8,x1,#1; mov x0,#3; bl foo   not a constant: the callee alone
      g     mov x8,#0x38; svc #0; bl lib      lib calls foo before anything
                                              else, so its body proves
                                              nothing: the constant alone
      g2    mov x8,x1; svc #0; bl cancel      the glibc helper shape: cancel
                                              writes x8 before its own svc and
                                              returns without reading it
      use   mov x8,sp; mov w0,#7; bl mk       the control: mk stores through
                                              x8, a real hidden return
      use2  mov x8,sp; mov w0,#9; bl mkthunk  mkthunk is `b mk`, a tail call
                                              into a body that reads x8

    A second chunk holds clang -O0 code for
      static int report(int code, int line, int msg);   (calls nothing)
      static int corrupt(int line) { return report(11, line, 7); }
      static unsigned pagecount(void *p) { return ((unsigned *)p)[16]; }
      int cursor(void *bt, unsigned pg) { if (pg <= 1) { if (pg < 1)
        return corrupt(4321); if (pagecount(bt) == 0) pg = 0; }
        return (int)pg; }
    where `w8 = pg - 1` reaches both calls.  pagecount never takes x8, and
    the value vetoed there must not become corrupt's argument instead.  A
    third chunk holds callees that DO take x8: `helper` is `svc #0; ret`
    (the kernel reads the caller's syscall number) and `cnt` loops on x8.
    A fourth holds `phibuf`, which computes `add x8,sp,#16` once, calls the
    leaf `pleaf` on one path, recomputes it and joins into `pmk`, a callee
    whose body calls out first.  The frame address is a leftover at pleaf
    and the real result buffer at pmk, so dropping it at pleaf must not
    drop it at pmk.

    Pass 1 (`option hiddenretarg off`) reproduces the BUG: `foo(0x38,3)`,
    `foo(a1 + 1,3)`, `lib(0x38)` and `cancel(a1...)`.
    Pass 2 (`option hiddenretarg on`, the shipped default) is the FIX: no call
    carries x8, while both struct returns keep their result buffer.
-->
